= Ideosphere — Simple =

The '''ideosphere''' is a metaphor for the environment in which ideas spread, change, compete for attention, and interact. It is often compared with the biosphere: instead of organisms and genes, the metaphor imagines ideas or “memes” circulating through culture.

The concept is associated especially with Douglas Hofstadter's discussions of viral ideas in the 1980s and with the broader memetics tradition influenced by Richard Dawkins.<ref name="hofstadter">Hofstadter, D. R. (1985), ''Metamagical Themas'', especially “On Viral Sentences and Self-Replicating Structures.”</ref><ref name="dawkins">Dawkins, R. (1976), ''The Selfish Gene''.</ref>

The ideosphere is '''not an established physical place or field'''. It is a way of thinking about cultural transmission.

== What the metaphor notices ==

Ideas really can:

* spread from one person to another;
* remain stored in books, websites, institutions, and archives;
* be changed when they are retold;
* become more or less popular;
* support or conflict with other ideas;
* travel through communication networks.

These processes can be studied scientifically without assuming that ideas literally behave like organisms.

== Ideas are not genes ==

Biological genes are copied through specific molecular mechanisms. Ideas can be transmitted in much less regular ways.

People can deliberately rewrite an idea, combine several ideas, misunderstand what they hear, independently invent something similar, or reject a message after considering it.

Because of this, words such as “mutation,” “selection,” and “fitness” are analogies unless a researcher clearly defines how they are being measured.

== Memetics and cultural evolution ==

'''Memetics''' treats memes as cultural replicators. It became influential in popular and philosophical discussion, but the meme is difficult to define as a precise unit and memetics has not become a universally accepted science of culture.

Modern cultural-evolution research studies social learning, transmission, population effects, and cumulative culture using mathematical models, experiments, anthropology, archaeology, and other methods.<ref name="cultural">[https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/evolution-cultural/ Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Cultural Evolution.”]</ref>

Researchers can therefore study cultural evolution without treating the whole culture as a literal ideosphere.

== Media and institutions matter ==

Ideas do not spread only because of their content. Schools, publishers, governments, religions, companies, social networks, search engines, and recommendation systems affect which ideas people encounter.

An idea can become popular because it is useful or true, but also because it is easy to remember, emotionally powerful, repeatedly promoted, or algorithmically amplified.

'''Popularity is not evidence of truth.'''

== Related concepts ==

The [[Semiosphere]] is a different theory about cultural sign systems and translation. The [[Infosphere]] concerns informational environments more broadly. [[Information ecology]] examines relationships among information, people, technologies, and institutions.

An idea is also not automatically [[Knowledge]]. A false claim can spread extremely well.

== Researching idea spread ==

A careful study can define one particular idea or variant, identify a population or collection of documents, record when and where versions appear, and test possible transmission pathways.

A network diagram can show who was connected to whom, but it cannot by itself prove what an idea meant or whether one person copied another.

